      <h1 class="article__title">
        EES Says Your Trip Is NOT OK. You Disagree. Here's How to Find Out
        Who's Right.
      </h1>

      <p class="article__meta">October 22, 2026 · 8 min read</p>

      <p class="article__audience">
        <strong>This post is for you if:</strong> the EU's official stay
        check, or a border officer, has given you fewer days than your own
        count, and you want to know whether the record is wrong and how to
        get it fixed.
        <br/>
        <br/>
        <strong>It's probably not necessary if:</strong> your own count and
        the official one agree.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="article__body">
      <p>
        You're planning your next trip to the Schengen Area. You've kept
        careful track of your days, and by your count you have 40 left. Then
        you run the EU's official stay check, and it reports a different
        number, or it says NOT OK.
      </p>

      <p>
        With the rollout of EES came an official tool for checking the
        compliance of an upcoming trip. While the number is official, it can
        be wrong. Some travellers have reported the EES calculator, and border
        officers reading from it, as inflating their previous stays without
        explanation.
      </p>

      <p>
        Errors in the record can happen, and thankfully they can be fixed. But
        the official number isn't always wrong either. Before you contest
        anything, you need to work out whose count is off. This guide walks
        you through that check, then covers what proof to gather, what to say
        at the border, and how to get your record corrected.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>What is the EES?</h2>

      <p>
        The Entry/Exit System (EES) is the EU's electronic border record. It
        replaced passport stamps for most short-stay visitors to the Schengen
        Area. Each time you cross the external border, your entry or exit is
        logged against your passport, face and fingerprints, which the system
        uses to calculate your 90/180 compliance.
      </p>

      <p>
        That means the record is only as accurate as the crossings in it. If
        one is missing, duplicated or logged on the wrong date, any verdict
        offered by the EES is potentially poisoned.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can see the system's answer through the EU's{" "}
        <a
          href="https://travel-europe.europa.eu/ees/check-how-long-you-can-stay_en"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Check how long you can stay</a
        > tool. It gives you an OK or NOT OK and the number of days you have
        left, but at the time of writing, it does not show you the individual
        entries and exits behind that number.
      </p>

      <h2>Why your count and the EES might disagree</h2>

      <p>
        Most mismatches come from one of four causes. Two are errors in the
        record. The others are differences in how you count.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>An exit wasn't recorded.</strong> This is the big one. If
          EES never logged you leaving, it assumes you're still in the
          Schengen Area and keeps counting. Exits have gone unrecorded when
          officers fell back to a quick manual passport check to clear a
          queue, during equipment faults, and at smaller land crossings. Once
          your authorised stay runs out with no exit on file, the system flags
          you as an overstayer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A crossing was recorded twice or on the wrong date.</strong>
          A duplicate entry, or a crossing logged against the wrong day
          inflates your recorded stay.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You're referencing different dates.</strong> The EU tool
          asks for your intended arrival or departure date, and answers as of
          that date. A border officer may tell you how many days you'll have
          left at your planned exit. If you're comparing your count today
          against an answer for a date weeks away, the two may not match
          without either being wrong. The{" "}
          <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window"
            >rolling 180-day window</a
          > moves with the date you inspect.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You're counting entry and exit days differently.</strong>
          Both your entry day and your exit day count as full Schengen days,
          however late you arrived or early you left. If your own count leaves
          them out, you'll be short by up to two days per trip. More on that
          in{" "}
          <a href="/blog/does-your-entry-day-count-as-a-schengen-day"
            >Does your entry day count as a Schengen day?</a
          >
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What kind of error is it?</h2>

      <p>Work through these in order.</p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Rebuild your trips from documents.</strong> Pull up boarding
          passes, booking confirmations and passport stamps. Re-enter every
          crossing from the last 180 days with the dates they show.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Count entry and exit days as part of your stay.</strong> If
          your total moves when you do this, that was part of the problem.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Count to the same date the official answer used.</strong>
          Run your count as of the same arrival or departure date you gave the
          EU tool, or the date the officer used.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Look at the size of the difference.</strong> If your
          corrected count and the official one still differ by a day or two,
          look again at steps 2 and 3. If they differ by weeks, roughly the
          time since one of your departures, or the time between two of your
          trips, then a missing exit is the likely cause. If they differ by
          about the length of a single trip, look for a crossing recorded
          twice.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        This is easier with a calculator that shows its steps.{" "}
        <a href="/app">EuroVisaCalculator</a> lists every trip and every day
        it counted, so you can see exactly which days make up your total and
        line them up against the official answer. Other tools can give
        different answers for the same dates, which is covered in{" "}
        <a href="/blog/why-schengen-calculators-give-different-results"
          >Why Schengen calculators give different results</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>A worked example</h2>

      <p>
        Anna, a Canadian, visits Spain from 10 to 24 April (15 days) and Italy
        from 10 June to 14 July (35 days). Unbeknownst to Anna, her exit from
        Italy on 14 July was not recorded. She plans to arrive in France on
        1 September.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Her own count:</strong> 50 days used in the 180 days before
        1 September. She has 40 left.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>What EES reports:</strong> As far as the system knows, she has
        been in the Schengen Area since 10 June. Add the 15 April days and she
        hit 90 on 23 August. EES has flagged her as an overstayer since then,
        and the stay check for 1 September returns NOT OK.
      </p>

      <p>
        The gap between the two answers is about seven weeks, which lines up
        with the time since her July departure. That points to the missing
        exit, tells her which country to contact (Italy), and tells her which
        documents to dig out.
      </p>

      <h2>Gather your proof</h2>

      <p>
        Passport stamps are no longer routine, so proving you left is now
        largely on you. For the crossing in dispute, collect anything that
        places you outside the Schengen Area on or right after the date you
        say you left:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Boarding passes and e-tickets for the flight, train, ferry or bus
          out.
        </li>
        <li>
          Entry stamps from the country you went to next. A UK, Turkish or
          Albanian entry stamp dated the day you left is strong evidence you
          were no longer in the Schengen Area.
        </li>
        <li>Any Schengen stamps an officer did put in your passport.</li>
        <li>
          Accommodation or car hire outside the Schengen Area from your exit
          date onward.
        </li>
        <li>
          Card or bank statements showing purchases in a non-Schengen country.
        </li>
        <li>
          Your own trip log, with dates and crossing points, tying it all
          together.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Keep this for every trip, not just the disputed one, and keep it for
        years. An uncorrected record without an exit is held for five years.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you travel as a couple or a family, each person has their own EES
        record. One of you can be wrongly flagged while the others are fine,
        so check everyone's status separately and keep proof for each person.
      </p>

      <h2>What to say at the border</h2>

      <p>
        If an officer tells you that you've overstayed or are not allowed to
        enter, stay calm and be specific.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Name the crossing you think is wrong.</strong> Give the
          date, the airport or crossing point, and the direction.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Show your proof.</strong> Have your boarding pass and the
          onward country's entry stamp ready before you're asked.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ask the officer to check the entry and exit records, not
          just the days-remaining figure.</strong> That's where a missing exit
          shows up.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ask for the record to be corrected.</strong> The UK
          government's advice to its travellers is to approach border
          officials at your next crossing and request a correction.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>If you're refused entry, ask for the decision in
          writing.</strong> It names the authority that made it and how to
          appeal.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        For business travellers, the practical rule is simple: carry the proof
        for your latest trips on your next one, especially if your schedule
        doesn't leave room for a delay at the border.
      </p>

      <h2>How to get your EES record corrected</h2>

      <p>
        The EES Regulation gives you the right to see the data held about you
        and to have inaccurate data corrected (Article 52 of Regulation (EU)
        2017/2226). You don't need to be in Europe to use it.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Who to contact.</strong> You can send your request to the
        competent authority of any country that uses the EES. The country
        whose border handled the crossing in question is usually the best
        choice, because it can check its own data. The EU's{" "}
        <a
          href="https://travel-europe.europa.eu/ees/data-held-by-ees"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Data held by the EES</a
        > page lists the authority and contact details for each country.
        Several national police and border services publish their own request
        forms.
      </p>

      <p><strong>What to send.</strong></p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Your full name, date of birth, nationality and passport number, with
          a copy of your passport.
        </li>
        <li>
          A statement that you are requesting access to your EES data and
          rectification of an inaccurate record under Article 52 of Regulation
          (EU) 2017/2226.
        </li>
        <li>
          The crossing that is missing or wrong: date, place and direction.
        </li>
        <li>Copies of your proof.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Ask for access and correction in the same request. You don't need to
        see your file before asking for it to be fixed.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>How long it takes.</strong> The authority has to answer within
        45 days of receiving your request. If you wrote to a country that
        isn't responsible for the record, it has 30 days to check the data
        where it can do so on its own. At the time of writing, at least one
        authority has said that high request volumes mean it may take the
        full period.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>What a correction does.</strong> Where an error made you look
        like an overstayer, the regulation requires your data to be removed
        from the overstayer list and the record corrected (Article 35).
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>If nothing happens.</strong> Follow up in writing once the 45
        days are up. If you still get no answer, or a refusal you disagree
        with, you can complain to that country's data protection authority or
        take the matter to its courts.
      </p>

      <p>
        A pending request doesn't give you any extra days, and the next
        officer may see the same flag until the correction goes through. It's
        best to sort it out before your next trip if you can.
      </p>

      <p>
        If the record isn't corrected, it can have real consequences on your
        next trip. In a future blog article we will cover what those are.
      </p>

      <h2>Check your own count first, every time</h2>

      <p>
        The EES gives you a number but it doesn't show you how it was
        calculated. A calculator that shows how it arrived at its answer gives
        you something concrete to hold up against the official record: every
        trip, every counted day, and the exact point where the two diverge.
        That's what you need to know whether to fix your own count or contest
        theirs. <a href="/app">Check your days with EuroVisaCalculator</a>,
        and see{" "}
        <a href="/blog/how-we-source-and-verify-visa-data"
          >how we source and verify our visa data</a
        >.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <h3>Does a NOT OK from the EU stay check mean I'll be refused entry?</h3>
      <p>
        Not automatically. A border officer makes the entry decision. But
        treat a NOT OK you don't expect as a reason to check your record
        before you travel.
      </p>

      <h3>Can I see which of my crossings EES has recorded?</h3>
      <p>
        Not through the public stay check. You need to make a formal access
        request to a national authority under Article 52.
      </p>

      <h3>Which country should I contact about a missing exit?</h3>
      <p>
        You can contact any country that uses the EES, but the one where your
        exit should have been recorded can usually check and correct it
        fastest.
      </p>

      <h3>How long does a missing exit stay on my record?</h3>
      <p>
        If it's never corrected, five years from the date your authorised stay
        ran out. A normal record is kept for three years.
      </p>

      <h3>My partner's EES count is fine but mine is wrong. Why?</h3>
      <p>
        Each traveller has a separate record. A crossing can be logged
        correctly for one person and missed for another.
      </p>

      <h3>They skipped my fingerprints when I left. Is my exit missing?</h3>
      <p>
        Not necessarily. When biometric checks are paused, your passport is
        still checked and your crossing is still recorded.
      </p>

